  ('qudt:CT_COUNTABLY-INFINITE', 'foundation:lastUpdatedAt', NULL, '2026-03-04T04:10:29Z', 'xsd:dateTime', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 208, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_COUNTABLY-INFINITE', 'http://purl.org/dc/terms/description', NULL, '
  A set of numbers is called countably infinite if there is a way to enumerate them. 
  Formally this is done with a bijection function that associates each number in the set with exactly one of the positive integers.
  The set of all fractions is also countably infinite.
  In other words, any set $X$ that has the same cardinality as the set of the natural numbers,
   or $| X | \; =  \; | \mathbb N | \; = \; \aleph0$, is said to be a countably infinite set.
  ', 'qudt:LatexString', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_COUNTABLY-INFINITE', 'http://www.linkedmodel.org/schema/dtype#literal', NULL, 'countable', 'xsd:string', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_COUNTABLY-INFINITE', 'qudt:informativeReference', NULL, 'http://www.math.vanderbilt.edu/~schectex/courses/infinity.pdf', 'xsd:anyURI', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_COUNTABLY-INFINITE', 'rdf:type', 'qudt:CardinalityType', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'iri', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_COUNTABLY-INFINITE', 'rdfs:isDefinedBy', 'http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'iri', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_COUNTABLY-INFINITE', 'rdfs:label', NULL, 'Countably Infinite Cardinality Type', 'xsd:string', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_FINITE', 'foundation:lastUpdatedAt', NULL, '2026-03-04T04:10:29Z', 'xsd:dateTime', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 208, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_FINITE', 'http://purl.org/dc/terms/description', NULL, 'Any set $X$ with cardinality less than that of the natural numbers, or $| X | \\; <  \; | \\mathbb N | $, is said to be a finite set.', 'qudt:LatexString', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_FINITE', 'http://www.linkedmodel.org/schema/dtype#literal', NULL, 'finite', 'xsd:string', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_FINITE', 'rdf:type', 'qudt:CardinalityType', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'iri', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_FINITE', 'rdfs:isDefinedBy', 'http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'iri', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_FINITE', 'rdfs:label', NULL, 'Finite Cardinality Type', 'xsd:string', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_UNCOUNTABLE', 'foundation:lastUpdatedAt', NULL, '2026-03-04T04:10:29Z', 'xsd:dateTime', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 208, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_UNCOUNTABLE', 'http://purl.org/dc/terms/description', NULL, 'Any set with cardinality greater than that of the natural numbers, or $| X | \; >  \; | \mathbb N | $,  for example $| R| \; =  \;  c  \; > |\mathbb N |$, is said to be uncountable.', 'qudt:LatexString', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_UNCOUNTABLE', 'http://www.linkedmodel.org/schema/dtype#literal', NULL, 'uncountable', 'xsd:string', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_UNCOUNTABLE', 'rdf:type', 'qudt:CardinalityType', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'iri', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_UNCOUNTABLE', 'rdfs:isDefinedBy', 'http://qudt.org/2.1/schema/qudt', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'iri', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CT_UNCOUNTABLE', 'rdfs:label', NULL, 'Uncountable Cardinality Type', 'xsd:string', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CardinalityType', 'foundation:lastUpdatedAt', NULL, '2026-03-04T04:10:29Z', 'xsd:dateTime', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 208, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CardinalityType', 'http://purl.org/dc/terms/description', NULL, '
  In mathematics, the cardinality of a set is a measure of the number of elements of the set.
  For example, the set $A = {2, 4, 6}$ contains 3 elements, and therefore $A$ has a cardinality of 3. 
  There are two approaches to cardinality: one which compares sets directly using bijections and injections,
   and another which uses cardinal numbers.
  ', 'qudt:LatexString', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CardinalityType', 'qudt:informativeReference', NULL, 'http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cardinal_number', 'xsd:anyURI', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CardinalityType', 'qudt:informativeReference', NULL, 'http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cardinality', 'xsd:anyURI', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CardinalityType', 'qudt:plainTextDescription', NULL, 'In mathematics, the cardinality of a set is a measure of the number of elements of the set.  For example, the set ''A = {2, 4, 6}'' contains 3 elements, and therefore ''A'' has a cardinality of 3. There are two approaches to cardinality – one which compares sets directly using bijections and injections, and another which uses cardinal numbers.', 'xsd:string', NULL, 'literal', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
  ('qudt:CardinalityType', 'rdf:type', 'owl:Class', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'iri', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, 201, 0, 0),
